{
  format: 1
  name: install-move-into-one-of-two-same-named-collections
  suite: scenario
  spec: ["#modules", "§13.16", "§13.2"]
  note: '''
    The attack is a package shaped so that a written destination could plausibly
    name TWO module collections. The root declares a top-level `modules` map of
    `module` values AND a row-scoped `companies.modules` of the same declaration
    name — both legal, and §13.2 makes them genuinely distinct ("Installing the
    same package in each space creates two independent instances").

    A module collection is an ORDINARY collection, so `<-` addresses it through
    the language's own collection references and nothing about modules is special:
    `/modules[@name]` is the package-root one and `.companies[@id].modules[@name]`
    is the row-scoped one. There is no module-space coordinate to translate into,
    so there is nothing to translate wrongly.

    The check is a discrimination, not a smoke test. Each install must populate
    EXACTLY ONE of the two and leave the other empty: a resolution that confused
    them would report the same success while putting the instance somewhere the
    operator did not ask for, and reading only the collection you expected would
    look correct. Both are read after each install, and the two packages carry
    different seeds (5 and 9) so a collision could not hide behind equal values.
  '''

  packages: {
    host: {
      $liasse: 1
      $app: "t.modinstall.samename@1.0.0"
      $model: {
        modules: {
          $key: "text"
          $value: "module"
          $interfaces: { items: { $view: { $key: "id", id: "text", qty: "int" } } }
        }
        companies: {
          $key: "id"
          id: "text"
          modules: {
            $key: "text"
            $value: "module"
            $interfaces: { items: { $view: { $key: "id", id: "text", qty: "int" } } }
          }
        }
        $mut: {
          "provision_root({ name: text, package: blob })": [
            "/modules[@name] <- unpack(@package)"
          ]
          "provision_row({ company: text, name: text, package: blob })": [
            ".companies[@company].modules[@name] <- unpack(@package)"
          ]
        }
        $public: {
          root_items: { $view: '/modules["sales"]::items' }
          acme_items: { $view: '/companies["acme"].modules["sales"]::items' }
        }
      }
      $data: { companies: { acme: {} } }
    }
    root_sales: {
      $liasse: 1
      $module: "t.modinstall.samename.rootsales@1.0.0"
      $model: { items: { $key: "id", id: "text", qty: "int" } }
      $data: { items: { a: { qty: "5" } } }
      $expose: { items: { $view: ".items { id, qty }" } }
    }
    row_sales: {
      $liasse: 1
      $module: "t.modinstall.samename.rowsales@1.0.0"
      $model: { items: { $key: "id", id: "text", qty: "int" } }
      $data: { items: { a: { qty: "9" } } }
      $expose: { items: { $view: ".items { id, qty }" } }
    }
  }
  root: host

  steps: [
    // The package-root spelling populates the package-root collection only.
    { module_lifecycle_call: { mutation: "provision_root", package: "root_sales"
        args: { name: "sales" } }
      expect: { outcome: ok } }
    { watch: "public.root_items", id: "wr1", expect_init: { value: [ { id: "a", qty: "5" } ] } }
    { watch: "public.acme_items", id: "wa1", expect_init: { value: [] } }

    // The row-scoped spelling populates the row's collection only, and the seeds
    // differ, so neither read could be the other's instance.
    { module_lifecycle_call: { mutation: "provision_row", package: "row_sales"
        args: { company: "acme", name: "sales" } }
      expect: { outcome: ok } }
    { expect_view: { watch: "wa1", value: [ { id: "a", qty: "9" } ] } }
    { expect_view: { watch: "wr1", value: [ { id: "a", qty: "5" } ] } }
  ]
}
